A classic example of a lock-free data structure is the "Wait-Free Queue" (also known as the "Lock-Free Queue" or "Non-Blocking Queue").

Here's a simplified implementation in C:
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>

struct Node {
    int value;
    struct Node* next;
};

struct WaitFreeQueue {
    struct Node* head;
    struct Node* tail;
    atomic(int) size;

    void enqueue(int value) {
        struct Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
        newNode->value = value;
        newNode->next = NULL;

        while (1) {
            // Check if the queue is empty
            if (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&tail, &head, newNode)) {
                continue; // retry
            }

            // Check if the new node can be inserted at the tail
            if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&newNode->next, &NULL, newNode)) {
                break;
            }
        }

        size++;
    }

    int dequeue() {
        while (1) {
            struct Node* temp = head;

            // Check if the queue is empty
            if (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &temp, NULL)) {
                continue; // retry
            }

            // Check if we can remove the node from the head
            if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&temp->next, &NULL, temp)) {
                break;
            }
        }

        size--;
        return temp->value;
    }
};
```
This implementation uses atomic operations to ensure that the queue is accessed in a lock-free manner. The `enqueue` function checks for two conditions:

1. If the queue is empty, it sets the new node as the tail and updates the head.
2. If the new node can be inserted at the tail without blocking other threads, it breaks out of the loop.

The `dequeue` function works similarly, checking if the queue is empty and if we can remove the node from the head without blocking other threads.

This implementation ensures that the queue operations are wait-free, meaning that no thread will block indefinitely waiting for another thread to release a lock.
